WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.142
The Attacks On September 11, 2001, American Airlines Flight 11 took off from Boston at 7:59 in

2
00:00:02.143 --> 00:00:11.292
the morning. Moments after the seat-belt sign was switched off, two men got up from the second row,

3
00:00:11.293 --> 00:00:21.288
approached two female flight attendants, and stabbed them. More hijackers broke into the cockpit, overpowering the pilots.

4
00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:25.954
In just minutes the five hijackers of Flight 11 had gained full control of the plane and

5
00:00:25.955 --> 00:00:34.025
its passengers. September 11 started as an ordinary morning for the thousands of men and women who worked

6
00:00:34.026 --> 00:00:45.090
in the twin towers of the World Trade Center, the tallest skyscrapers in New York City. But

7
00:00:45.091 --> 00:00:51.169
all that changed at 8:46, when Flight 11 slammed into the north tower. The plane ripped through

8
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:59.415
seven floors, sending the tower swaying and instantly killing hundreds of people. These five hijackers weren't working alone.

9
00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:12.072
About 15 minutes later, a second hijacked plane, United Airlines Flight 175, slammed into the south tower.

10
00:01:12.073 --> 00:01:21.241
As people frantically raced to escape the burning buildings—some even jumping from windows—rescue personnel rushed to the

11
00:01:21.242 --> 00:01:27.649
site. Almost all of Lower Manhattan was evacuated, creating confusion on the streets. News of the disaster spread

12
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:38.800
quickly. At schools and workplaces all over the world, people stopped what they were doing to turn

13
00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:44.062
on the television or call loved ones. Then, at 9:59, the south tower collapsed. Half an hour later,

14
00:01:44.063 --> 00:01:51.617
the north tower collapsed too, shrouding Lower Manhattan in dust and smoke. Soon news stations were reporting

15
00:01:51.618 --> 00:01:57.353
the fates of two more hijacked planes. One had flown into the Pentagon, the nation's military headquarters

16
00:01:57.354 --> 00:02:03.621
in Washington DC. The other plane, headed for either the White House or the Capitol building, had crashed

17
00:02:03.622 --> 00:02:15.427
in rural Pennsylvania after a brave attempt by passengers to take back control. By ten thirty that

18
00:02:15.428 --> 00:02:23.586
morning, nearly three thousand people had perished, with thousands more injured or missing. New York City's skyline

19
00:02:23.587 --> 00:02:39.664
had been drastically altered; Washington DC was in chaos; and people all over the world were stunned and

20
00:02:39.665 --> 00:02:42.426
terrified. 9/11 had become a day that changed the world. Who Was Behind the Attacks? Investigators soon

21
00:02:42.427 --> 00:02:53.336
uncovered strong evidence that a Muslim extremist named Osama bin Laden and his terrorist group, al-Qaeda, were

22
00:02:53.337 --> 00:02:59.702
responsible for the attacks. American president George W. Bush responded by declaring a global war on terrorism. This

23
00:02:59.703 --> 00:03:09.065
war was aimed at bin Laden and al-Qaeda as well as at the Taliban, a militant Islamic

24
00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:17.870
group that had taken over most of Afghanistan. The American government believed that the Taliban was supporting

25
00:03:17.871 --> 00:03:26.879
bin Laden and al-Qaeda and sponsoring terrorist training camps. As Muslims, bin Laden and the members of al-Qaeda

26
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:32.805
follow the religion of Islam, along with over one billion other people in the world. But as

27
00:03:32.806 --> 00:03:41.870
extremists, they believe that American life is offensive to the teachings of Islam. In their eyes it

28
00:03:41.871 --> 00:03:48.282
is the duty of Muslims to destroy American people and ways of life through violent and extreme means.

29
00:03:48.283 --> 00:03:51.520
This is very different from the messages of peace and kindness practiced by most Muslims. The U.S.

30
00:03:51.521 --> 00:03:58.689
and the World Respond On October 7, 2001, the U.S. and its allies attacked Kabul, Afghanistan. The

31
00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:03.846
purpose of the attack was to capture bin Laden and bring down the Taliban. Over the next two

32
00:04:03.847 --> 00:04:11.751
years, a total of 53 countries joined the U.S. in its fight against terrorism. President Bush also

33
00:04:11.752 --> 00:04:17.250
announced the creation of the Department of Homeland Security. Working with other federal agencies, this department tightened security

34
00:04:17.251 --> 00:04:27.404
along America's borders as well as inside airports and other public places. Government employees were put in

35
00:04:27.405 --> 00:04:35.919
charge of screening baggage, and passengers had to pass through metal detectors and have their luggage X-rayed.

36
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:41.481
People all over the world were no longer allowed to carry sharp objects on flights. In addition the

37
00:04:41.482 --> 00:04:46.051
U.S. government passed the PATRIOT Act. This act gave more freedom to law enforcement agencies to search

38
00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:57.547
people's telephone and email communications as well as their medical, financial, and library records. It also granted

39
00:04:57.548 --> 00:05:06.128
immigration authorities more power to detain and deport immigrants suspected of terrorism-related acts. Many people strongly objected to

40
00:05:06.129 --> 00:05:10.731
this expansion of government power. They were concerned that it would lead to discrimination of immigrants and

41
00:05:10.732 --> 00:05:17.886
violate people's rights to privacy. Ground Zero The area where the twin towers had collapsed became known

42
00:05:17.887 --> 00:05:19.616
as Ground Zero. Workers and volunteers spent months here, searching for victims among smoking mounds of rubble. Each

43
00:05:19.617 --> 00:05:25.196
day trucks and barges transported thousands of tons of material from Ground Zero to a nearby landfill

44
00:05:25.197 --> 00:05:32.034
so workers could sift through the debris for human remains as well as evidence from the planes.

45
00:05:32.035 --> 00:05:46.104
For many workers this was a difficult and upsetting task. After the areas of the attacks were cleaned

46
00:05:46.105 --> 00:05:51.093
up, it was time for rebuilding. At the Pentagon the damaged section of the building was restored

47
00:05:51.094 --> 00:05:56.265
within a year and a memorial was built a few miles away. In Pennsylvania, at the field

48
00:05:56.266 --> 00:06:04.823
where the fourth plane crashed, the government decided to build a memorial—a chapel with forty wind chimes to

49
00:06:04.824 --> 00:06:18.162
honor the plane's forty passengers and crew members. It took a long time to decide what to

50
00:06:18.163 --> 00:06:22.588
do about the World Trade Center. Some people thought the towers should be rebuilt exactly as before,

51
00:06:22.589 --> 00:06:28.481
like the Pentagon. Others felt the site and its ruins should become a monument to the people who

52
00:06:28.482 --> 00:06:36.228
died that day, like the field in Pennsylvania. In the end it was decided to erect one

53
00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:44.213
new tower and place a memorial where the old towers had once stood. After Darkness . . .

54
00:06:44.214 --> 00:06:47.213
Light On September 11, 2001, 19 hijackers committed the worst acts of terrorism ever aimed at the

55
00:06:47.214 --> 00:07:00.040
United States, changing that country—and the world—forever. Each year on September 11, Americans find ways to remember

56
00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:09.113
the victims. Many people attend memorial services. Children often wear red, white, and blue to school—the colors of

57
00:07:09.114 --> 00:07:19.441
the American flag—and raise money for projects that help others in their communities. In a town in

58
00:07:19.442 --> 00:07:26.846
Pennsylvania, some victims' family members decided to honor their loved ones by building a garden. At this

59
00:07:26.847 --> 00:07:36.435
garden, completed in 2006, visitors can take a memorial journey to remember all those who were killed on

60
00:07:36.436 --> 00:07:42.823
9/11. The journey starts in a tear-shaped area and winds past fragments from the twin towers. Then

61
00:07:42.824 --> 00:07:47.081
it passes a wall etched with the victims' names. Finally the walkway leads visitors away from these

62
00:07:47.082 --> 00:07:56.003
signs of sorrow to a cluster of young trees that stand for hope. The theme of this journey

63
00:07:56.004 --> 00:08:06.808
captures America's struggle to find hope following the tragedy—its theme is "After Darkness . . . Light."

